---
import ArticleSchema from "../components/ArticleSchema.astro";
import AuthorByline from "../components/AuthorByline.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  fullTitle="Shanghai Restaurant Reservations: Booking Guide"
  description="Guide to Shanghai restaurant reservations: how to book a table at top restaurants, when to reserve, and tips for getting into the city's most popular spots."
>
  <main>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-20 md:pt-28 pb-12">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl tracking-tighter leading-none font-semibold text-slate-900">Book Your Shanghai Restaurant Experience</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 mt-6 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">Securing a table at Shanghai's best restaurants can be competitive. Here is how to navigate the reservation landscape like a pro.</p>
        <AuthorByline />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
      <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-8">
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">When to Book</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">Michelin-starred restaurants: 2-4 weeks ahead. Popular casual spots: 3-7 days. Weekends and holidays: add 1-2 extra weeks. Hairy crab season (Sep-Dec): book as early as possible.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">How to Book</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">Most restaurants accept bookings via <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dianping" rel="noreferrer" class="text-accent-600 hover:text-accent-700">Dianping</a>, Meituan, or direct phone calls. Dianping — which Wikipedia describes as "a Chinese technology company that operates a platform for local services" with "consumer reviews under the Dazhong Dianping brand" — is where most locals check ratings before reserving. International visitors can use Chope or email the restaurant directly. Some high-end spots require a deposit.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Walk-In Strategy</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">For no-reservation spots like Jia Jia Tang Bao, arrive 15-30 minutes before opening. Lunch rush is 11:30-13:00; dinner is 18:00-20:00. Avoid peak times for shorter waits.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Booking as a Foreigner</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">You do not need to speak Chinese to reserve. Most Michelin-listed and hotel restaurants answer email in English, and your hotel concierge can place the call for you in minutes. For the Dianping and Meituan apps — the platforms locals use — screenshots of the restaurant page let any staff member book from the listing even if you cannot read the interface. Chope still lists a handful of international-style venues with English-only booking.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Deposits and No-Shows</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">Fine-dining and tasting-menu restaurants increasingly take a deposit of roughly RMB 200-500 per head, charged to a card or via WeChat, and forfeited entirely for a no-show. Casual and mid-range venues rarely require anything. Confirm the cancellation window when you book — most high-end kitchens ask for at least 24 to 48 hours' notice and will hold your table for fifteen minutes past the booking time before releasing it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-soft bg-white shadow-tinted p-6">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">A Phrase to Save</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">If you would rather phone it in yourself, memorize one line: <em>"Wǒ xiǎng dìng yī gè wèizi, X yuè X hào wǎnshang Qī diǎn, liǎng wèi"</em> — "I'd like to book a table, [date], 7pm, for two." Lead with the date, time, and party size; ask for a window or private room (<em>kāchā</em> / <em>bāoxiāng</em>) only after the table is confirmed. Speaking slowly with those three details is enough to book almost anywhere in the city.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <a href="/" class="btn-soft bg-accent-600 hover:bg-accent-700 text-white text-sm font-medium px-6 py-3 transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2">Browse Top Shanghai Restaurants</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <ArticleSchema
    headline="Book Your Shanghai Restaurant Experience"
    description="Guide to Shanghai restaurant reservations"
    url="https://www.shanghai-restaurant.com/reservations"
  />
</Layout>
